this facility was an old mine  it was operated for about 125 years  and these elevators are basically a mile  in depth  and now we're in the process of  converting and operating this as a deep  underground science facility really  trying to unlock the secrets of the  universe  in the middle of south dakota's black  hills lies the sanford underground  research facility  here physicists are conducting  experiments that have the potential to  alter our understanding of how the  universe was created  our lab is unique in that we are the  deepest underground facility for doing  this type of work in the united states  really the goal is to try and limit any  sort of impurities that can get into the  experiment that would cause problems  with their data collection  we're a mile underground because that  layer of rock and dirt protects us from  cosmic rays that are impinging on the  surface of the earth a number of cosmic  rays down here is effectively zero  especially compared to the surface  one of our experiments that we have in  the lab is trying to unlock questions  about particles that are fairly new in  our understanding of the universe like  neutrinos  in any given second about 60 billion of  them pass through your thumbnail  the irony is that this experiment  studies neutrinos without ever detecting  one  we are looking for a radioactive decay  that can only take place if the  neutrinos play a certain role in there  we collect about a terabyte of data a  week  you couldn't have taken this much data  let alone analyzed it 20 years ago so  it's very exciting from that point of  view  it is the team's hope that this  abandoned mine which once took from  earth will now help us understand our  place in the universe  if we understand the neutrino we can  potentially understand why the galaxies  and the stars and the planets evolved  the way they did and why they're here  we have one nobel prize under our belt  already and we're hoping that the  experiments that we have here now will  lead to additional nobel prizes in the  future  you
 

